---
/**
 * The privacy policy (T80.11), linked from the Play listing, rewritten for the public apps (D64):
 * no telemetry; updates, downloads and Sign in with GitHub go to github.com; search by meaning
 * downloads its model from Hugging Face once; Send feedback uses the visitor's own GitHub token.
 * Keep docs/android.md's Play Data safety answers in step, and change the date with the text.
 */
import SiteLayout from '../components/SiteLayout.astro';
import { REPOSITORY } from '../site';
---

<SiteLayout
  title="Privacy policy · Penmoss"
  description="Penmoss has no server, no account and no analytics. Your notes stay on your devices and in a git repository you choose."
>
  <article class="wrap prose">
    <h1>Privacy policy</h1>
    <p class="meta">Effective 7 November 2026</p>

    <p>
      Penmoss is a free, open source notes app for the Mac, Android and the command line. It is made
      by Ryan Henderson, an individual developer, not a company (&ldquo;the developer&rdquo; below).
      &ldquo;You&rdquo; means anyone who uses the apps or this website.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>In short.</strong> Penmoss has no server and no account. Your notes stay on your devices
      and in a git repository that you choose and control. The developer does not receive your
      notes. There are no ads, no analytics and no tracking, in the apps or on this website, and
      nothing is sold or shared for anyone else&rsquo;s use.
    </p>

    <h2>Where your notes are</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>On your devices.</strong> Each app keeps a copy of your notebooks: notes, folders, tags,
        daily and weekly notes, attached files, version history, comments and settings. It keeps them
        in its own private storage, so they open offline.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>In your git repository.</strong> Each notebook is a git repository (or a folder in one)
        that you add yourself. The apps sync with it using your git host&rsquo;s own sign-in: a token
        you give, your Mac&rsquo;s git credentials, or Sign in with GitHub. Your git host stores the
        notes under its own terms and privacy policy. Penmoss does not check whether the repository
        is private: if you choose a public repository, anyone can read your notes there.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Version history</strong> is the repository&rsquo;s commit history. Each change records
        which device or agent made it, with the commit name and email you set.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Penmoss is not end-to-end encrypted: your notes are stored in your repository as files your git
      host can read.
    </p>

    <h2>What leaves your device</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Sync</strong> goes only to the git repositories you added, over HTTPS or SSH.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>GitHub.</strong> When you add a notebook on the Mac, the app asks GitHub which repositories
        you can reach, and can create one for you. Sign in with GitHub talks to github.com directly.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Updates and downloads.</strong> The Mac app checks for updates on GitHub Releases, and
        the download links on this website go to GitHub. GitHub sees your IP address, under its own
        terms.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Search by meaning</strong> on the Mac downloads its model once from Hugging Face. After
        that it runs on your Mac: no note text leaves it to search.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Send feedback</strong>, when you choose to use it, files an issue in <a
          href={REPOSITORY}>Penmoss&rsquo;s public GitHub repository</a
        > with your own GitHub token. The issue holds what you write, the screenshots you attach and
        a short description of the screen and app version, but never note text unless you put it
        there. It is public, under your GitHub account, and GitHub stores it under its own terms.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>AI agents you connect</strong>, such as Claude Code through Penmoss&rsquo;s command-line
        tool and local MCP server, can read and change the notebook you connect them to. They run on
        your Mac, and their providers handle what they read under their own terms. Penmoss never
        does this on its own.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Pictures from other websites.</strong> When a note shows a picture by its web address,
        your device downloads it from that website, which sees your IP address. On Android you can turn
        this off in Settings.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Penmoss sends no analytics, no crash reports and no usage data to the developer or anyone else.
      This website sets no cookies and loads nothing from any other site.
    </p>

    <h2>The developer&rsquo;s own relay</h2>
    <p>
      The developer runs one small service on Cloudflare, the relay, so that Claude on the web and on
      mobile can reach the developer&rsquo;s own notebook. It signs in only the developer&rsquo;s
      GitHub account and serves only that one repository. It never handles anyone else&rsquo;s
      notes.
    </p>

    <h2>What the apps keep on your devices</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Mac:</strong> your notebooks in the app&rsquo;s storage; the tokens you give, encrypted
        with a key in the macOS Keychain; and the search-by-meaning model and its index, in
        <code>~/.penmoss</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Android:</strong> your notebooks, attached files and downloaded web pictures in the
        app&rsquo;s private storage, and your tokens encrypted with a key held in the Android Keystore.
        None of it goes into Android&rsquo;s cloud backup or device-to-device transfer. When the app
        asks for your fingerprint, face or screen lock before a change, Android checks it on the phone;
        Penmoss never receives it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Command line:</strong> its notebooks under <code>~/.penmoss</code>, and its tokens in the
        system keychain or a private settings file.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>Removing an app removes these copies from that device. Your repository keeps the notes.</p>

    <h2>Deleting your data</h2>
    <p>
      Deleting a note moves it to Trash. Delete for good removes it from your notebook. Earlier
      versions stay in the repository&rsquo;s history until you rewrite or delete the repository.
      Because the repository is yours, you can delete it, or anything in it, at any time with your git
      host.
    </p>

    <h2>Security</h2>
    <p>
      Every connection uses HTTPS or SSH. On the Mac and Android, deleting for good and other
      sensitive changes need Touch ID, a fingerprint or the device&rsquo;s password first. Your git
      host decides how your repository is protected at rest. To report a security problem, see <a
        href={`${REPOSITORY}/security/policy`}>the security policy</a
      >.
    </p>

    <h2>Children</h2>
    <p>
      Penmoss is not meant for anyone under 18 and does not knowingly collect data from children.
    </p>

    <h2>Changes to this policy</h2>
    <p>
      If the way Penmoss handles data changes, this page is updated first and its effective date
      changes with it. Its history is in <a href={`${REPOSITORY}/commits/main/site/src/pages/privacy.astro`}
        >the repository</a
      >.
    </p>

    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p>
      For questions, open an issue on <a href={`${REPOSITORY}/issues`}>GitHub</a>, or use the developer
      contact email on Penmoss&rsquo;s Google Play listing.
    </p>
  </article>
</SiteLayout>
